1. Introduction
• Overview of Helicopter Aerodynamics:
A helicopter is a very complex flying machine which unlike conventional fixed wing aircraft does not relay of fixed geometry to generate lift and turbomachine to generate propulsive force but rather a single rotor arrangement to fulfill all purposes of propulsion, lift force and maneuvering capabilities with precise control over the configuration of the rotor. This induces many aerodynamic non-linearities in the system in terms of instability, adverse torsional forces, gyroscopic effects and wake interactions causing turbulence and vibration impacting the noise and thus the acoustics study for enhanced efficiency also plays a vital role. Generally, two broad phases of flight in rotorcrafts include the hovering condition and foreword flight condition. The main purpose of development of rotorcrafts of any kind in the history of mankind was the need for stationary aerial vehicle in the air which does not need foreword flight motion to produce lift force i.e., hovering condition corresponds to the major performance criteria for efficiency in rotorcrafts and thus a deeper understanding of this state of the rotor’s aerodynamics is essential in the progress for better aerial systems and performance study.
• Brief Geometric Description of Model:
Broadly speaking the need for tandem type configuration is the impact on counter torque management and reduce adverse impacts of vertical/horizontal rotor blade interactions like in a classical helicopter or chopper layout. Tandem rotor systems, consists of two rotors in parallel planes of rotation separated by some horizontal separation (HS) i.e., perpendicular to the axis of rotation and vertical separation (VS) i.e., parallel to the axis of rotation. They also possess a phase angle (j) i.e., which is defined as the angle made between the lines joining the rotor hub and the rotor tip of both. For the purpose of this study, the geometric configurations are varied in the ranges of HS = 1.3R to 2.8R, VS = 0.2R to 0.4R and j = 0° to 150°, to have a comparative study of the performance and flow character giving rise to potential explanations for the variation due to near vicinity aerodynamic phenomenon. For the sake of clarity and detail, the rotors are a combination of NACA 23024 at the root section and NACA 23012 at the tip section with a collective pitch of +18° in the clockwise direction and -18° in the counter clockwise direction for the upstream side and downstream side rotors respectively.
• Overview of the Method and Software:
To explore these effects, a study has been undertaken to investigate the aerodynamic performance parameters of the tandem rotors in hover conditions (zero advance ratio) at 5000 RPM using an open-source, meshless solver based on the Vortex Particle Method: DUST (DAER Unsteady Simulation Technique), developed by Airbus and Politecnico di Milano which are validated against experimental findings.


