DUST is a mid-fidelity, flexible software used to simulate the aerodynamics of complex, nonconventional aircraft configuration. It uses a “Lagrangian” based approach where the vortex particles are traced, once shed from the vortex lattice or the surface panels on the geometry throughout the void space of the fluid domain to compute the flow field i.e., the velocity and pressure field employing specifically the vortex particle method implemented via the Blade Element Theory (BET) and the Blade Element Momentum Theory (BEMT) models encapsulating the “Lifting Line” to accurately model a rotor blade with specific airfoil cross section using the lifting line data such as the coefficient of lift and drag to compute the required data without having to actually model the complete geometry of the blade of rotors saving on the computational time but yet delivering consistent results.
• Overview of Experimental Methods:
To experimentally obtain the performance and acoustics data, many different tools and methods were employed and the results of which provide a key validation to the results shown by the simulations which includes thrust and torque measurements done using load and torque cells, induced velocity and angle of attacks measurements done using pressure transducers and pitot probes, vorticity and flow velocity field measurements done using particle induced velocimetry techniques (PIV), sounds level intensity and pressure fluctuation measurements done using microphones and sound level meters in an anechoic chamber.
The load and torque cells consist of strain gauges placed strategically in a configuration to capture the change in length caused due to the force or moment, resistance changes from which can then be calibrated to show the loads or torque values when mounted on a rigid structure to dampen the ambient disturbances. The pressure transducer consists of sensing elements to produce a potential difference converted to digital impulse based on the difference in pressure which is then calibrated to show the flow induced velocity and pressure in the vicinity of the rotor.
The PIV setup consists of a light source (class-4 laser), high speed camera, synchronizer and seeding particles generator arrangement to capture instantaneous and time averaged images of the flow field by tracing the particles in spatially locked frames to corelate them thus determining the flow field velocity in the plane of interest. The acoustic measurement includes microphones and sound level meters to capture the intensity and pressure fluctuations in near and far fields of the rotor at different azimuth and radial distances.


