2. Fundamentals of Helicopter Aerodynamics
A helicopter is a very complex flying machine which unlike conventional fixed wing aircraft does not relay of fixed geometry to generate lift and turbomachine to generate propulsive force but rather a single rotor arrangement to fulfill all purpose of propulsion, lift force and maneuvering capabilities with precise control over the position of the rotor blades achieved using a rotor slip disc arrangement for variable pitch, lag and yaw controls both collectively and individually. To meet these demands, one needs a deeper knowledge on the aerodynamic environment of the rotor and the aerodynamic loading effects on the dynamic response on rotor. The lifting capability of the rotating blade is related to its local angle of attack (AoA) and the dynamic pressure associated to that section. For hovering flight conditions, the velocity profile along the blade span is considered linear radially and azimuthally axisymmetric with zero flow velocity at the rotational axis to maximum velocity Vtip at the blade tip.
The local dynamic pressure at any blade element is proportional to the square of the distance from rotational axis. Thus, for a constant pitch/AoA of the blade, it experiences a thrust force proportional to the Vtip2 and power proportional to the Vtip3. For foreword flight conditions, the velocity experienced by the blade elements will stay linear but will no longer stay axisymmetric as different azimuth positions of the blade will experience a relative velocity that will increase or decrease the net local velocity and as a result also vary the net lift force distribution, thrust force and the power consumption.
