4. The BET, BEMT Models and Vortex Particle Numerical Methods
• Blade Element Theory:
It is the basis for the more complex developed models of a helicopter rotor (or propellor) which provides a quantitative and adequate results of the forces and moment distribution along the span of the blade and thus the averaged or integrated loads over the rotor disk as a whole can be computed. It assumes each blade section as a quasi-2D airfoil section which individually produce the force and moment which is used to compute rotor performance as a whole and serves as a primary design consideration in terms of the blade twist or dihedral and the planform area for maximized rotor performance.
It does not directly account for the induced velocity and the induced AoA on each section but can be assumed to follow a distribution which can be imposed in the solution obtained from BET to get the induced effects and also the 3-D effects at the tips. These difference in the distribution of induced velocity arises due to the complex phenomenon of the vortical wake interacting with the rotating rotor blade in the disk plane. The Biot-Savart law is also used to calculated the influence of vortical wake structures and the induced AoA.
