Its core formulation includes the following:
- Instead of solving the Navier-Stokes equations on a fixed grid (Eulerian approach), VPM relies on the vorticity formulation of the Navier-Stokes equations. - The vorticity, which represents the local swirling motion of the fluid, is discretized into a collection of particles (vortices). - These particles move and interact with each other, their movement governed by the velocity field they induce and the free-stream velocity. - The velocity field is reconstructed from the vorticity distribution using mathematical relationships, like the Biot-Savart law for incompressible flows.
It holds an advantage over the classical Eulerian approach in terms of the less complexity dues to meshless based solving, low numerical dissipation and capturing or core vortex features without loss in accuracy, efficient in dealing with high vorticity and unsteady problems and ease in tracking the flow field features i.e., greater adaptability to change in flow features and interactions. This formulation can be solved using the fast multipole method (fmm) for the flow domain as defined by the particle box limits, the framework for which is discussed in the subsequent sections.


