• Fast-Multipole Method (FMM):
The Fast Multipole Method (FMM) is an algorithm that efficiently calculates long-range interactions between many particles, reducing the computational complexity from quadratic to nearlinear time (O (N) or O (N log N)). It achieves this by grouping nearby particles into cells and approximating their combined effect on distant particles using multipole expansions.
This drastically speeds up calculations, especially for large systems, by avoiding direct pairwise computations for all particles. By efficiently combining near and far field calculations using multipole and local expansions, FMM significantly reduces the computational cost of simulating systems with long-range interactions.
