5. DUST User Interface and Methodology
• DUST User Interface:
It uses a command prompt based FORTAN written source code to call functions and change parameters from pre-defined functions using the terminal window to initiate the input files for geometry, solver algorithm, and the references defined. It creates the output files in the form of data (.dat) and plot (.plt) formats which can be loaded in software like Excel and Tecplot for further post processing and computation.
• DUST Pre-processor:
It is called in the terminal window with the command “dust_pre.in” which is the input file tag given to the preprocessor which runs the sequence of commands as defined using the following files to fulfill the purpose of geometry file (.h5) generation:
Input File: This is the main root file of the pre-processor with the name “dust_pre.in” which calls the required geometry definition, mesh generation, referencing system and the airfoil table files within it to pass on the read data to the directory file of the DUST which generates the geometry (.h5) file in the same parent path as the input file for the solver to then deploy its solver and compute the necessary solution data files. It has the following syntax of commands:
- comp_name: defines the name given to the component used in referencing in solver - geo_file: calls the files path of the file discussed in (b) subpart - ref_tag: gives a reference tag name to component - file_name: saves the generated file with the extension .h5 for solver to refer to
(In case of the single rotor configuration, only 1 component is defined i.e., 2 blades of the rotor and no hub is considered for simplicity as it does not contribute to the thrust or torque significantly. In case of the tandem rotor configuration, only 2 components are defined i.e., 2 blades of the upstream rotor and 2 blades of the downstream rotor)
a) Geometry Definition Files: These are the input (.in) format files that the DUST preprocessor reads when requested for via the input file (.in) called through the terminal. It includes the data of the different blade sections which will be implemented in the geometry and their definition on the basis of root, tip and other sectional chord lengths, spans, angle of twists, dihedral angles, and other relevant parameters.
b) References Files: These are the input (.in) format files that the DUST preprocessor reads when requested for via the input file (.in) called through the terminal. This defines the reference frames which can be of the type moving or stationary with subtypes of rotating and translating based on


