Input File: This is the main root file of the pre-processor with the name “dust_post.in” which calls the required result files within it to pass on the read data to the directory file of the DUST which generates the data (.dat) and visualization (.vtk) file in the Post_Processed_Data folder parent path as the input file for further post processing. It has the following syntax of commands:
- data_basename: specifies the file path of the raw data files from the simulation result - basename: specifies the file path of the result post processed files stored in the “Post_Processed_Data” folder in the parent folder
- Analysis = {type} defines the type of analysis performed on the result files i.e., visualization (of the particles and wake field), flowfield (of the fluid domain whole), sectional loads and moments, integral loads, probe data, aeroacoustics data - Analysis = {variables} defines the variable we require to compute for the different analysis - Analysis = {format} defines the format of the output files generated - Analysis = {average} defines the variable computed is over the total simulation average or just for the particular time step data
(In case of both simulation result analysis, the sectional and integral loads were set with the necessary reference frame settings as shown to analyze the aerodynamic performance and the particle visualization along with the flow field visualization is stored in tecplot processing files. The aeroacoustics data is also computed for the desired time step rotations at the steady state condition after 11 rotations which will give the modal acoustics on the surface of the blade geometry)
a) Visualization Files: These are the visualization (.vtk or .plt) format files that the DUST postprocessor generates when requested for via the input file (.in) called through the terminal. During the computation of the velocity and pressure data on the vortex particles, it the computes the spacial position and trajectory of each particle in reference to the position of release in the global coordinate system and maps it into the above file formats which can be then viewed and processed using appropriate software like Paraview or Tecplot. It holds the pressure and velocity data of the particles along with the velocity vectors and coefficient of pressure.
b) Flow Field Files: These are the flow field (.vtk or .plt) format files that the DUST postprocessor generates when requested for via the input file (.in) called through the terminal. From the particle data computed above, the DUST uses the “Fast Multipole Method” (fmm, a mathematical tool to create sampling boxes around the particles to compute the velocity and pressure field by either interpolation or averaging the vicinity particles) to calculate the velocity and pressure field in the fluid domain, technically termed as the “Octree Box” in the software. These boxes are customized for their length and growth in the respective directions based on the resolution requirements and accuracy along with the degree of the fmm used to generate them which directly corelates to the computational cost and time complexity of the program.


