c) Sectional Loads and Moments Files: These are the data (.dat) format files that the DUST postprocessor generates when requested for via the input file (.in) called through the terminal. As the software inculcates a provision for specified airfoil section rotor or propellor defined by the section along its span, it also delivers the sectional loads and moments along the requested axis with respect to the progressing time step for each blade component. This is in the local rotating frame of reference which can be corelated to the global loads and moments using the appropriate rotation matrix for that section based on its definition and orientation in the global frame.
d) Integral Loads and Moments Files: These are the data (.dat) format files that the DUST postprocessor generates when requested for via the input file (.in) called through the terminal. It also provides the integral values of the net load and moment acting on the components with reference to the position in global coordinate system defined in the References.in file read by the pre-processor. It also acts as a check for the consistency in data captured by the sectional loads which on numerical integration (by appropriate method) should yield in the approximate values as the integral loads.
e) Aeroacoustics Files: These are the data (.dat) format files that the DUST postprocessor generates when requested for via the input file (.in) called through the terminal. The pressure and velocity fluctuations caused on the solid body surface studied as “Modal Acoustics” are the data computed by the software as a sectional fluctuation with each time step of both pressure and velocity. These surface data can then be fed into appropriate static or harmonic acoustic analysis software to impose the FWH model to compute the far-field or near field acoustic data at a distinct receiver location.
6. Experimental Setup and Methodology
For the below discussed parameter measurements experimentally, the BLDC motor rated with KV was used in addition to 30 Amp Electronic Speed Controller (ESC) to control the rotational speed of the motor which is measured using an infrared RPM sensor. These electronics are integrated via the “Arduino” microcontroller for ease of code implementation and control. The code algorithm for motor speed control and RPM measurement are attached in the annexure.
• Thrust and Torque Measurements:
Thrust and Torque measurements were achieved using an in lab built load and torque cell fabricated using aluminum sheets to form a S-type structure such that the thrust and torque transfer from the motor to base mount happens via the cells which houses strain gauges connected to a signal amplifier board and a Wheatstone bridge to measure the change in resistance caused due to the strain over the differently strain gauges strategically placed over the cell to get an average reading which is calibrated using standard weights to give a thrust and torque value.


_____________________Aerodynamic and Aeroacoustics Performance of Tandem Rotors
28
• Induced Velocity and AoA Measurements:
Induced Velocity and AoA measurements were achieved using an in lab developed 8 channel differential pressure sensors connected using polyvinyl flexible silicone tubing to pitot tubes mounted on a frame construction just downstream of the rotor to measure the stagnation pressure and thus compute the dynamic pressure and the velocity along the blade span at different rotational speed.
• Vorticity and Velocity Field Visualization:
Vorticity and Velocity Field were achieved using a PIV setup assembled in lab with its complex systems of Laser, High speed camera and the synchronizer for accurate timing. The post processing of the captured images was done through PIVLab (standalone software from MATLAB) to obtain vector velocity field after a cross-correlation is achieved between the 2 frames of capture to estimate the velocity of the flow field.
• Sound Pressure Level and Intensity Measurement:
Acoustic measurements were achieved using sound level meters to find the near and far field regions for the rotor and then microphones were used to capture the sound intensity and pressure fluctuation in the anechoic chamber as a series of time function which is post processed to get the frequency domain data using Fast Fourier Transforms.


